    <div class="jr-answer">
      <h2>The short answer</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>For skin, hair and nails, marine collagen is the better pick.</strong> It is close
        to pure type 1, the same collagen that makes up roughly 80% of your dermis, and its smaller
        peptides are reported to absorb about 1.5 times more efficiently than bovine. Bovine gives
        you type 1 and type 3 together and costs less per gram, but it comes from cattle hide.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Source:</strong> marine collagen comes from fish skin and scales, usually cod, tilapia or snapper. Bovine collagen comes from cow hide and bone.</li>
        <li><strong>Types:</strong> marine is 90% or more type 1. Bovine is a type 1 and type 3 mix, which is why it gets sold for gut and connective tissue as much as for skin.</li>
        <li><strong>Dietary fit:</strong> beef-derived bovine collagen is a hard no for a large share of Indian buyers. Marine sidesteps that, though neither one is vegetarian.</li>
        <li><strong>Honest limitation:</strong> no adequately powered trial has ever put marine against bovine on the same skin measurements in the same people. The absorption edge is inferred from peptide chemistry, not proven in a head-to-head study.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

