      <h2 id="what-is-different">Marine vs bovine collagen: what is actually different?</h2>

      <p>
        The difference starts with the animal and ends up touching everything else. Marine collagen
        is extracted from fish skin and scales. Bovine collagen is extracted from cattle hide, bone
        and connective tissue, which are byproducts of the beef industry. The collagen types you
        get, the peptide size, the price, the religious question: all of it traces back to that one
        fact.
      </p>

      <p>
        Collagen is the structural protein that holds you together. It is the most abundant protein
        in the human body, roughly a third of your total protein, and it forms the scaffolding in
        skin, tendon, bone, cartilage and blood vessel walls. Every animal makes it. Which animal
        yours came from decides what kind you are swallowing.
      </p>

      <p>
        Both marine and bovine collagen sold as supplements are <strong>hydrolysed</strong>, which
        means the long triple-helix strands have been cut enzymatically into short fragments called
        peptides. Unhydrolysed collagen is a 300,000 Dalton molecule that your gut simply
        dismantles into generic amino acids. Hydrolysis is the line between a supplement and
        expensive gelatin, and I have gone through that mechanism properly in
        <a href="/pages/collagen-peptides">collagen peptides: what they are and how they work</a>.
      </p>

      <p>
        One term worth defining, because it appears on half the listings on Indian marketplaces.
        <strong>Grass fed collagen</strong> means bovine collagen from cattle raised on pasture
        rather than grain. It tells you about the cow's diet. It tells you nothing about peptide
        size, dose, or whether the product will do anything for your skin. It is a farming label
        wearing a lab coat, and it is often used to make the beef origin sound wholesome rather
        than to disclose it.
      </p>

