      <h2 id="types">Which collagen types do you get from each?</h2>

      <p>
        Marine collagen is almost entirely type 1. Bovine collagen is a mix of type 1 and type 3,
        usually around 80:20 depending on the tissue it was extracted from. Neither contains
        meaningful type 2, the cartilage collagen, and that matters a great deal if joints are your
        reason for buying.
      </p>

      <p>
        There are 28 known collagen types and three of them do the work you care about.
        <strong>Type 1</strong> is the skin, tendon and bone collagen, and it accounts for roughly
        80 to 90% of the collagen in your dermis. <strong>Type 3</strong> sits alongside type 1 in
        skin, blood vessels and the gut wall, and it is proportionally more abundant in younger
        skin. <strong>Type 2</strong> is cartilage-specific and comes from chicken sternum, not
        from fish or cattle. The full breakdown is in
        <a href="/pages/types-of-collagen">types of collagen: 1 vs 2 vs 3 explained</a>.
      </p>

      <p>
        So the type argument favours marine for skin, but not by the margin marine brands imply.
        Type 3 is not filler. It is a legitimate dermal collagen and bovine supplies it, which is a
        point in bovine's favour that rarely gets made. The stronger case for marine is not the type
        ratio at all. It is the peptide chemistry, which is the next section.
      </p>

      <p>
        The type argument does have one clear loser. If you bought a bovine powder for knee pain
        because the label said it supports joints, you bought type 1 and 3 for a type 2 problem.
        Different ingredient, different dose, different evidence base, all of it covered in
        <a href="/pages/collagen-for-joint-pain">collagen for joint pain</a>.
      </p>

