      <h2 id="absorption">Is marine collagen absorbed better than bovine collagen?</h2>

      <p>
        Probably yes, and the reason is molecular weight. Fish collagen has lower thermal stability
        and a slightly different amino acid arrangement than mammalian collagen, so it breaks into
        smaller peptides during hydrolysis. Comparative work summarised by Subhan and colleagues
        puts marine collagen peptide uptake at roughly 1.5 times that of bovine and porcine
        peptides.
      </p>

      <p>
        Here is why size decides the outcome. Iwai and colleagues showed in 2005 that specific small
        fragments, Pro-Hyp and Hyp-Gly, survive digestion intact and appear circulating in human
        blood after a dose of collagen hydrolysate. Those fragments seem to act as signals rather
        than as bricks, reaching the dermis and prompting fibroblasts, your collagen-making cells,
        to increase output. Peptides below roughly 2,000 Daltons cross the gut wall efficiently.
        Above about 5,000, much less does.
      </p>

      <p>
        Marine collagen starts closer to that threshold, so less processing is needed to get it
        there. Bovine can be hydrolysed to the same molecular weight, and good bovine products are.
        It just costs the manufacturer more to do it, which is exactly why the cheapest bovine tubs
        say "hydrolysed collagen" and then go quiet about the molecular weight.
      </p>

      <p>
        Now the caveat, and it is a real one. That 1.5 times figure comes from peptide uptake and
        pharmacokinetic work, not from a trial where one group took marine, another took bovine, and
        somebody measured their skin twelve weeks later. <strong>That trial does not exist at any
        useful size.</strong> Anyone telling you marine is clinically proven superior to bovine is
        extrapolating from chemistry, and so, if I am being straight with you, am I.
      </p>

