      <h2 id="verdict">The verdict, by what you are trying to fix</h2>

      <p>
        Choose by goal rather than by brand story. Marine wins for skin, hair and nails. Bovine is
        perfectly reasonable if you want general collagen protein and have no objection to beef. For
        joints, neither one is the right answer on its own.
      </p>

      <h3>If your goal is skin, hair or nails</h3>
      <p>
        Go marine. You want type 1, the smaller peptides, the faster absorption, and you want it
        paired with vitamin C rather than taken naked. The fuller case sits in
        <a href="/pages/marine-collagen">marine collagen: benefits, dose and how to choose</a>, and
        the underlying skin evidence in <a href="/pages/collagen-for-skin">collagen for skin</a>.
        For hair specifically, keep expectations calibrated: collagen supports the follicle
        environment and supplies keratin building blocks, and it does not regrow hair the way
        minoxidil does.
      </p>

      <h3>If your goal is general protein or connective tissue</h3>
      <p>
        Either works and bovine is cheaper, so take the cheaper one. Just understand what you have
        bought. Collagen is an incomplete protein, missing tryptophan entirely, so it is no
        substitute for dal, paneer, eggs or whey if your real problem is total daily protein. That
        distinction is worked through in
        <a href="/pages/collagen-vs-whey-protein">collagen vs whey protein</a>.
      </p>

      <h3>If your goal is joints or knees</h3>
      <p>
        Neither marine nor bovine type 1 is the first-line choice. The joint trials that produced
        the clearest results used undenatured type 2 collagen at 40mg daily, a different ingredient
        at a different dose from a different animal. Buying type 1 marine powder for knee pain is a
        category error, and an expensive one.
      </p>

      <h3>If beef is off the table</h3>
      <p>
        Then the decision is already made for you. Marine, or a builder blend that supports your own
        synthesis with vitamin C, zinc and amino acids while you accept that the evidence for those
        is weaker. Do not let a grass fed claim muddy it. Grass fed is still cow.
      </p>

      <p>
        One last thing that outranks this entire comparison. Daily broad-spectrum sunscreen spares
        more collagen than either source can rebuild, and under Indian sun that margin is not
        small. If you are choosing between SPF and a collagen supplement, buy the SPF. If SPF is
        already a daily habit, then marine at 8g with vitamin C alongside it is the next step worth
        taking, and give it twelve weeks before you decide it failed.
      </p>

